Key Findings

1. Tension is now over‑detected. Under the refined definition (“sustained opposition”), all AIs found tension in the random sequence because of the erratic high‑low swings. This suggests the definition is still too broad – random fluctuations can mimic opposition.
   Recommendation: Tighten Tension to require structured opposition (e.g., regular alternation, competing attractors with memory) – not mere high‑low variability.
2. Split detection is inconsistent. Some AIs interpreted the initial drop from 96→83→19 as a qualitative asymmetry (branch). Others saw only a noisy fluctuation. The refined definition (“qualitative asymmetry”) may need an explicit threshold (e.g., a drop of >50% of range) to reduce ambiguity.
3. Scar and Decay were mostly absent (as desired), but a minority of AIs (Claude, Meta, Grok) incorrectly assigned Scar and even Decay (Meta). This indicates that the refined definitions still allow subjective interpretation.
4. Meta’s output is unusually complete (full cycle). It may be over‑interpreting random noise – a known issue with some models. This suggests that even refined definitions cannot fully eliminate model bias.

